From ued0b2a4a7f094dc3 at groups.google.com  Thu Nov 25 19:06:29 2021
From: ued0b2a4a7f094dc3 at groups.google.com (Francois R)
Date: Thu, 25 Nov 2021 19:06:29 +0000
Subject: Classic mode changes estimates with predictions, but not experimental mode
Message-ID: <NWjFx80CBxs.VsXwmOlYBQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/NWjFx80CBxs/m/VsXwmOlYBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello INLA team

I am building spatiotemporal models with SPDE and an AR1 weekly component. My response is a zero-inflated count and I use a zero-inflated negative binomial model. My locations can change from week to week and the number of locations for certain weeks can be quite small.

Like several others, I noticed that parameters estimates could change when adding NA responses for prediction. In my case, they can change so much as to make the values NaN, Inf or sd of basically 0 or simply make INLA crash and run for a very long time. One of the advice for those issues is to try the "experimental" mode, so I compared the "classic" and the "experimental" mode on a tiny subset of my data. In this version, my model probably has to many fixed effects and I use a very coarse mesh to reduce computation times. I use a stack for estimation and another for prediction and I don't think I have made a mistake in their definition.

See the two images below for the comparison. I'm showing the mean and 0.0275/0.975 quantiles for each hyper parameter in relation to the amount of locations to predict.  The 0 value is for a model without any value to predict (only the estimation stack is given). The classic mode shows a lot of variation and eventually parameter estimation does not work anymore (when dots are not included, it means values such as Inf or NaN were returned). The experimental mode however looks super stable (perhaps too stable!?). Everything was ran on a single core to reduce numerical issues. The fixed effects were a lot more stable in this particular case, although I have seen other cases were they were greatly affected as well.

Evidently, I prefer the experimental version, but how should we rely on it right now? Will it eventually become the defaut version? How can I interpret the classic version and is it indicative of something wrong with my model? As I said, the mesh I use is quite coarse (in relation to the expected range) and I noticed that using a finer mesh seemed to slightly improved the parameter estimation in the "classic" mode. This is with R 4.1.2 on Windows and with INLA 21.11.22. I will probably end up using inla.posterior.sample anyway since I have a lot of values to predict and it gives a bit more flexibility.

Thanks for your input and let me know if I should do further tests.
Francois Rousseu

