Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear R-INLA community:

Hello, I have been using R-INLA for a couple of years. For my own project, I employed several random slope models with and without spatiotemporally structured components from a dataset of 3,108 (the number of spatial entities) * 14 (the number of total time points):

form.m40 <- Mortrate ~
  f(GEOID, model = "bym", graph = graph, constr = TRUE) +
  f(year1, model = 'ar1', constr = TRUE) +
  f(GEOID1, UELAG1, model = "bym", graph = graph, constr = TRUE) +
  f(year2, UELAG1, model = 'ar1', constr = TRUE) +
  UELAG1 + PELDER + PNONWHITE + MEDINCOME + PUNIVABOVE + PPOVTBELOW + PRURAL + PRENTER + PHSINGLE
form.m4IV <- update(form.m40, ~ . + f(GEOID2, UELAG1, model = "bym", graph = graph, group = year3, control.group = list(model = "rw1"),
                          constr = TRUE) )

I used INLA version 21.03.20 (with PARDISO solver) to fit the model with form.m4IV, which took around 60 hours to get precise modes (mode.status==0) and wide marginal distributions. Recently I had a chance to rerun the models with the recent testing version of INLA (21.11.01), the model fit was notably improved that it just took six hours to attain preferable mode status (mode.status==0) with more concentrated marginal distributions than the those of previous model fit with the old version of INLA. Although I read through the package news and update records, I am not still very sure what made such an improvement. It can be guessed there was an improvement in the optimization module (e.g. "Smart Gradient" referred in R-INLA Project - News <https://www.r-inla.org/news#h.xxyhi43dddqw>) in the package. Does anyone have insights on the improvement of the model fitting procedure in recent versions of INLA?

Thank you very much.

